{"reply":"In Chapter 5, Alice’s changing size makes identity feel physically unstable: she cannot separate “who I am” from what has happened to her body. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are you?”, she answers, “I—I hardly know, sir, just at present,” because she knows who she was that morning but has “been changed several times since then.” She then makes the link explicit: “being so many different sizes in a day is very confusing.”

The uncertainty works in both directions. Her bodily changes make her question whether she is still herself, while her uncertain sense of self makes each new size feel like further proof that she has changed. Her wish to be “a little larger” also shows that size is not merely a physical problem: she treats a particular scale as necessary for feeling normal, even though the Caterpillar insists that three inches is “a very good height indeed.”

